(function () {
  window.CCA = window.CCA || {};
  window.CCA.lessonsNew = window.CCA.lessonsNew || [];
  window.CCA.scenarios = window.CCA.scenarios || {};
  window.CCA.why = window.CCA.why || {};
  window.CCA.questions = window.CCA.questions || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.CCA.lessonsNew.push(
    {"id": "integ-1", "n": 1, "domain": "integ", "title": "Taming tool and agent capability bloat", "body": "Every tool definition is text that Claude reads on each request. A catalogue of dozens of tools costs tokens, adds latency, and makes selection harder, especially when names and descriptions overlap. Bloat also applies to agents: a single agent holding every tool is harder to evaluate and has a larger blast radius when something goes wrong.\n\nThe remedies are architectural. Consolidate overlapping tools into fewer, well-described ones; give each tool a distinct purpose and a description that says when to use it. Return concise, high-signal results rather than raw payloads. Split a broad agent into focused subagents that each hold only the tools their role needs. For large catalogues, defer loading so that only a small set of hot tools is always present and the rest are discovered on demand.\n\nDo not prune by instinct. Measure which tools are used, how often the wrong one is chosen, and how many tokens definitions consume, then change one thing at a time against an eval set. Keep the tool set stable between requests where you rely on prompt caching, because changing definitions invalidates the cached prefix.", "points": ["Tool definitions are input tokens on every request, so catalogue size is a cost and latency decision as well as an accuracy one.", "Overlapping names and descriptions are a common cause of wrong tool selection; merge or sharpen them before adding more instructions.", "Tool results should be concise and high-signal; paging, field selection or a response-format parameter keeps context small.", "Role-focused subagents with narrow toolsets reduce both selection errors and the blast radius of a mistake.", "Deferred tool loading with a search tool lets a large catalogue exist without paying for every definition up front.", "Measure usage and selection accuracy first, then prune or defer against an eval set."], "traps": ["Assuming a larger model removes the need to curate tools, when selection quality still falls as overlapping tools multiply.", "Adding few-shot examples for every tool, which grows the prompt and treats a catalogue problem as a prompt problem.", "Swapping the tool list per request to save tokens, which breaks prompt caching and can cost more overall.", "Removing rarely used tools outright when deferring or role-based exposure would keep the capability."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/tool-search-tool", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"]},
    {"id": "integ-2", "n": 2, "domain": "integ", "title": "Authentication and authorization gaps in tool and agent chains", "body": "Authentication answers who is calling; authorization answers what that caller may do with this resource. In an agent system there are several callers: the human user, the application hosting the model, the MCP server or tool backend, and any downstream API. A gap appears when identity is lost along the chain, typically when a shared service account with broad rights performs every action regardless of who asked.\n\nThe sound pattern is to carry the end user's identity or a narrowed delegation of it to the backend, and to enforce permissions there, in code, on the resource. Instructions in a prompt are not an access control. For MCP servers reached over HTTP, the specification builds on OAuth and forbids token passthrough: a server must accept only tokens issued for itself, and obtain separate credentials for any downstream service, which avoids confused-deputy problems.\n\nApply least privilege per tool and per task: read and write capabilities separated, scopes narrow, tokens short-lived, secrets held server-side and never placed in the model's context. Sensitive or irreversible actions deserve server-side limits and human approval. Treat all tool results, such as ticket text or web pages, as untrusted input that may carry injected instructions.", "points": ["Identity must survive the whole chain; a broad shared service account hides who acted and over-grants access to every user.", "Authorization is enforced by the resource or backend in code, never by an instruction in the prompt.", "MCP over HTTP uses OAuth-style authorization; servers must validate that tokens were issued for them and must not pass client tokens downstream.", "Scope tools by least privilege: separate read from write, narrow scopes, short-lived tokens, secrets kept out of the model context.", "Subagents and delegated tasks should receive narrowed credentials, not the orchestrator's full rights.", "High-impact actions need preventive controls, such as server-side limits and approval, rather than after-the-fact logging alone."], "traps": ["Treating a system-prompt rule such as 'only refund small amounts' as an access control.", "Letting an MCP server forward the client's token to a downstream API because it is convenient.", "Relying on audit logs as the only control for irreversible actions; detective controls do not prevent harm.", "Placing API keys in tool arguments or prompts so the model can supply them."], "sources": ["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest/basic/security_best_practices", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/authorization"]},
    {"id": "integ-3", "n": 3, "domain": "integ", "title": "Justifying accuracy-latency trade-offs with evidence", "body": "Accuracy, latency and cost pull against each other. A larger model, more reasoning budget, more retrieved passages and extra verification steps usually raise accuracy but add time and spend. The architect's job is to turn the stated service level into numbers, then choose the cheapest configuration that meets both the quality bar and the latency target, and to show the evidence.\n\nStart by finding where time goes. In many systems it is not the model alone: it is sequential tool calls, large prompts, retrieval, or long outputs. Remedies differ. Streaming improves perceived latency for interactive users without changing total time. Parallelising independent tool calls and caching a stable prompt prefix cut real time. Routing sends easy requests to a smaller, faster model and escalates hard ones. Non-interactive work with no waiting user can use batch processing and trade latency for cost.\n\nBe careful with the dials that cost accuracy silently: truncating output with a low max_tokens, dropping a verification step, or cutting retrieval depth. Each change should be measured against a labelled eval set and recorded as a decision with its rationale.", "points": ["Convert the SLA into measurable targets for quality, latency percentile and cost before comparing configurations.", "Profile first; sequential tool calls, large prompts or retrieval are often the real latency source.", "Streaming lowers perceived latency; parallel tool calls and prompt caching lower actual latency.", "Routing by difficulty lets a smaller model serve simple requests and reserves a larger one for hard cases.", "Higher reasoning budgets and deeper retrieval help only for task classes where evals show a gain.", "Batch processing suits workloads where no user waits for each result."], "traps": ["Choosing the fastest model because the latency target is visible while quality is unmeasured.", "Using the largest model and maximum reasoning budget everywhere without checking the marginal gain.", "Cutting max_tokens to save time and causing truncated answers that look like quality failures.", "Treating streaming as a way to reduce total compute or cost."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing"]},
    {"id": "integ-4", "n": 4, "domain": "integ", "title": "Observability for agent systems at scale", "body": "An agent run is a tree of model calls, tool calls and sub-agent calls. Free-text application logs cannot reconstruct it. Observability for such systems means structured traces with a correlation identifier that links every step of a task, so that when a user reports a wrong answer you can see which prompt version, model, tool calls, arguments and results produced it.\n\nCapture for each step: latency, token usage, stop reason, tool name, outcome and retry count, plus version tags for the prompt, model identifier and tool set. These tags let you compare cohorts after a change and attribute a regression to its cause. Aggregate metrics should include percentiles such as p95 and p99, because averages hide the slow tail that users feel, and should be segmented by route, model and tenant.\n\nAt volume, recording every full payload is expensive and may hold personal data. Keep metrics for all traffic, sample full traces, and always retain traces for errors and low-scored outputs. Redact or hash sensitive fields, apply access control and retention limits, and involve your data protection officer where personal data is logged. Add online quality scoring on a sample, since a healthy status code says nothing about answer quality.", "points": ["Use structured traces with a correlation ID spanning orchestrator, subagents and tools rather than free-text logs.", "Tag each trace with prompt version, model identifier and tool set so changes can be compared.", "Track latency percentiles, error and retry rates, token usage and stop reasons, segmented by route and model.", "Sample full traces at scale but always keep errors and low-quality outputs.", "Redact sensitive data in logs and set retention and access rules; consult the data protection officer for personal data.", "Score a sample of live outputs against your rubric to detect quality drift that metrics miss."], "traps": ["Watching only average latency, which hides tail slowness.", "Logging everything verbatim with no redaction or retention policy.", "Treating a 200 response code as proof the answer was good.", "Sampling uniformly and discarding the rare failing traces you most need."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages"]},
    {"id": "integ-5", "n": 5, "domain": "integ", "title": "Designing a RAG pipeline: chunking and indexing", "body": "A retrieval-augmented generation pipeline has an offline path (parse, chunk, enrich, embed, index) and an online path (retrieve, optionally rerank, assemble context, generate). Most quality problems originate offline. Chunking decides what a retrievable unit is: chunks that are too small lose context, chunks that are too large dilute relevance and waste tokens. Structure-aware chunking that respects headings, clauses, tables and code blocks usually beats fixed-size splitting for structured documents; modest overlap protects ideas that straddle a boundary.\n\nEnrich chunks so they stand alone. Metadata such as document ID, version, effective date, section path and access labels supports filtering and citation. Contextual retrieval prepends a short, generated description of where the chunk sits in its document before embedding and keyword indexing, which Anthropic reports reduces failed retrievals, and reduces them further when combined with reranking.\n\nIndex hygiene matters as much as design. Use stable document and chunk IDs so refreshes upsert changed content and delete removed content; otherwise stale chunks survive and answers cite retired material. Embeddings from different models are not comparable, so a model change means re-embedding the whole index, ideally into a new index that is switched over once validated. Tune chunk size and top-k with a labelled retrieval eval set.", "points": ["Chunk along document structure where it exists; tune size and overlap against a retrieval eval set.", "Attach metadata for version, date, section and permissions to every chunk.", "Contextual chunk descriptions help isolated chunks match queries, at a one-time indexing cost.", "Use stable IDs so refreshes update and delete chunks, preventing stale or retired content from being retrieved.", "Re-embed the entire index when the embedding model changes, and switch over only after validation.", "Measure retrieval separately from generation, for example recall at k on labelled queries."], "traps": ["Tuning the generation prompt when the real fault is that the right chunk was never retrieved.", "Appending new documents on refresh while leaving superseded chunks in the index.", "Mixing vectors from two embedding models in one index.", "Splitting tables row by row so the headers are lost."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval", "https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/capabilities-contextual-embeddings-guide"]},
    {"id": "integ-6", "n": 6, "domain": "integ", "title": "Matching retrieval strategy to data shape and query pattern", "body": "There is no single best retrieval method. Choose by what the data looks like and how users ask. Free text answered by meaning suits embedding search. Exact identifiers, error codes, part numbers and rare terms suit keyword search such as BM25, so production systems often run both and merge the results, then rerank the merged list to put the best few passages in front of the model.\n\nStructured data, such as sales tables, belongs behind a query tool that runs SQL or an API call; embedding table rows cannot compute a total or a join. Relationship-heavy data, such as dependencies or organisation charts, is better served by graph or relational traversal. Source code often responds well to exact search and file reading driven by the agent, rather than a vector index that drifts from the repository.\n\nQuery pattern matters too. A one-shot lookup works with a single retrieval. Multi-hop questions, where the second search depends on the first answer, need an iterative or agentic loop. If the whole corpus is small and stable, skip retrieval infrastructure and place it in a cached prompt. Whatever you choose, evaluate retrieval on labelled queries drawn from real traffic categories.", "points": ["Semantic questions favour embeddings; exact-token queries favour keyword search; hybrid with reranking covers both.", "Aggregations and joins over structured data need a query tool, not vector search over rows.", "Graph or relational traversal fits relationship-centred questions.", "Multi-hop questions need iterative retrieval where later queries depend on earlier results.", "A small, stable corpus may fit in a cached prompt with no retrieval pipeline.", "Evaluate each strategy per query category, not on an average."], "traps": ["Using one vector index for every kind of data because it is already built.", "Expecting embeddings to match exact identifiers reliably.", "Increasing top-k to fix a wrong strategy, which adds noise and latency.", "Building retrieval infrastructure for a corpus that fits in context."], "sources": ["https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"]},
    {"id": "integ-7", "n": 7, "domain": "integ", "title": "Choosing between MCP, direct API or CLI, and agent-to-agent", "body": "Three integration mechanisms solve different problems. The Model Context Protocol standardises how an AI application (the host, with one client per server) connects to servers that expose tools, resources and prompts. Its value is reuse: write a server once and many MCP-capable applications can use it. A local server typically runs over stdio; a shared remote server uses Streamable HTTP with authorization.\n\nA direct API integration is a plain function in your application wrapping a service. For a single application with one stable backend, it is simpler and adds no server to operate. Command-line tools suit environments such as Claude Code, where well-known commands like git are already familiar to the model and add no schema overhead.\n\nAgent-to-agent protocols, such as A2A, address a different relationship: delegating work to an independent agent, often owned by another team or company, with discovery, task state and long-running status updates. They are not a replacement for tools. Subagents inside one application are usually better handled by your framework than by a network protocol. Decide by reuse, trust boundary, task duration and who operates the other side, and keep the number of moving parts proportionate.", "points": ["MCP fits capabilities that several AI applications should share through one standard server.", "A direct API wrapper is simpler when one application uses one stable backend.", "CLI tools suit coding-agent environments where commands are familiar and cheap in context.", "stdio suits local servers; Streamable HTTP suits shared remote servers and needs authorization.", "Agent-to-agent protocols suit delegation to independently operated agents with task lifecycle needs.", "Tools, resources and prompts are different MCP primitives with different controllers: model, application and user."], "traps": ["Building an MCP server for a single in-app function that nobody else will reuse.", "Using agent-to-agent protocols for what is really a stateless function call.", "Calling in-process subagents 'A2A' and adding network hops for no benefit.", "Assuming MCP removes the need for authorization and least privilege."], "sources": ["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest/basic/security_best_practices", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest/server/tools", "https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/a2a-and-mcp/"]},
    {"id": "integ-8", "n": 8, "domain": "integ", "title": "Progressive discovery versus monolithic context", "body": "A monolithic strategy places everything the model might need into the prompt up front. It is simple, predictable and, when the content is small, stable and always relevant, cheap if the prefix is cached. Its weaknesses grow with size: more tokens to pay for on every call, higher latency, and relevant facts diluted among irrelevant ones, which can degrade accuracy.\n\nProgressive discovery loads information in layers. The model sees a cheap index first, such as skill names and descriptions, a tool catalogue summary, file paths or document titles, and pulls details only when a task needs them. Agent Skills work this way: short metadata is present at start, the full instructions load when the skill is relevant, and bundled files are read on demand. Tool search applies the same idea to tool definitions, and just-in-time retrieval to documents. Subagents extend it by exploring in a separate context and returning a compact summary.\n\nThe cost is extra round trips and the risk that the model fails to look something up. Mitigate by keeping always-needed rules and hot tools in the stable prefix, writing descriptions that make it clear when to load more, and measuring both accuracy and latency. Use monolithic for the small core and progressive for the long tail.", "points": ["Monolithic context is fine for small, stable, always-relevant content, especially with prompt caching.", "Large or rarely needed content should be discovered progressively through a cheap index.", "Skills, tool search, just-in-time retrieval and subagents are all progressive-discovery mechanisms.", "Progressive loading adds round trips, so keep hot-path items in the stable prefix.", "Descriptions in the index must say when to load more detail, or the model will not look.", "Test both accuracy and latency; irrelevant context can lower accuracy as well as raise cost."], "traps": ["Putting an entire procedures manual in the system prompt because it fits in the window.", "Deferring everything, including the tools used on nearly every request.", "Writing vague index descriptions so the model never loads the detail.", "Assuming a larger context window removes the accuracy cost of irrelevant content."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/tool-search-tool", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"]}
  );

  window.CCA.scenarios.scIN1 = {"title": "Support copilot with a large MCP tool catalogue", "text": "A software vendor's support copilot connects through six MCP servers (CRM, ticketing, billing, knowledge base, deployment console, HR directory) to 48 tools. The agent acts for the signed-in support agent, can read customer records, and can issue account credits through the billing server. Tool definitions take a large share of each request, and the team sees the agent occasionally pick the wrong one of several similarly named tools. Today every call uses one shared service account with broad CRM access, and the billing MCP server forwards the token it receives to the billing API."};
  window.CCA.scenarios.scIN2 = {"title": "Knowledge assistant over a mixed manufacturing corpus", "text": "A manufacturer has indexed 30,000 documents: long policy PDFs with headings and numbered clauses, product specification tables, a parts catalogue with exact part numbers such as HX-4420-B, and change logs. Users ask conceptual how-to questions, exact part lookups, and comparisons across specification tables. Policies are revised weekly. In the pilot, answers often miss the relevant clause, part-number lookups return unrelated parts, and a retired policy version is sometimes cited."};
  window.CCA.scenarios.scIN3 = {"title": "Claims agents at scale with an external partner", "text": "An insurer runs an orchestrator that delegates to intake, fraud-check and payout subagents, handling about 200,000 claims a day. The adjuster desk needs replies within a few seconds, while overnight re-scoring of past claims has no waiting user. A loss-adjuster agent operated by a partner company on its own platform must assess photo evidence, a process that takes from minutes to hours. Current observability is free-text application logs."};

  window.CCA.questions.push(
    S("in001", "integ", "The copilot sometimes picks the wrong one of several similarly named tools, and tool definitions take a large share of each request. Which first step best addresses both problems?", ["Move to a larger model so it can tell the similar tool names apart", "Audit usage, merge overlapping tools, defer rarely used servers", "Add a worked example for each of the 48 tools to the system prompt", "Raise max_tokens so the model has room to reason about tool choice"], 1, "Overlapping tools and heavy definitions are a catalogue problem. Consolidating and deferring rarely used tools reduces both token cost and selection errors.", "scIN1"),
    S("in002", "integ", "Every copilot call uses one shared service account with broad CRM access. What is the most important change?", ["Log each call with the account name so misuse can be reviewed later", "Tell the model in the system prompt to read only the customer in the open ticket", "Rotate the service account password on a shorter schedule", "Carry the signed-in agent's identity to the CRM and enforce access there"], 3, "Authorization must be enforced by the resource using the real caller's identity. A shared broad account over-grants access and hides who acted.", "scIN1"),
    S("in003", "integ", "The billing MCP server forwards the token it receives from the client straight to the billing API. What is the right correction?", ["Accept only tokens issued for the MCP server itself and use a separate credential downstream", "Encrypt the forwarded token in transit and keep passing it through", "Shorten the token lifetime but keep the passthrough, since the client is trusted", "Move the forwarding step into the client so the server never handles the token"], 0, "MCP guidance forbids token passthrough. The server validates that tokens were issued for it and obtains its own downstream credentials, which avoids confused-deputy and audit problems.", "scIN1"),
    S("in004", "integ", "Credits are issued through the billing server. Which control best prevents an excessive credit, whether from model error or manipulation?", ["A system-prompt instruction capping every credit at the published policy limit", "A nightly report of credits above the limit for managers to review", "A server-side limit on the tool, with human approval above a threshold", "A second model call that judges whether the credit looks reasonable"], 2, "A preventive control enforced in code cannot be talked around. Prompt rules and after-the-fact reports do not stop the action, and a model judge is not deterministic.", "scIN1"),
    M("in005", "integ", 2, "Few sessions ever use the HR directory and deployment console servers. Which TWO changes reduce capability bloat while keeping those functions available?", ["Concatenate all tool descriptions into one long section of the system prompt", "Defer loading for tools from those servers so they are found through tool search", "Give every subagent the full set of 48 tools so routing is never needed", "Expose each server only to the roles that actually need it", "Raise the context budget so all definitions fit comfortably"], [1, 3], "Deferring loading and role-based exposure keep the capability but remove its standing cost and reach. The other options grow or merely hide the problem.", "scIN1"),
    S("in006", "integ", "Customers write the ticket text the agent reads, and the same agent holds the credit tool. What is the best design response to injected instructions in that text?", ["Add a prompt line telling the model to ignore instructions found in tickets", "Treat ticket text as untrusted, separate read from write, confirm writes", "Strip the word 'credit' from ticket text before the model sees it", "Rely on the model's training to resist injection and monitor for incidents"], 1, "Defence is structural: untrusted input, least privilege and a confirmation gate on state-changing actions. A prompt line or keyword filter is easy to bypass.", "scIN1"),
    S("in007", "integ", "Policy PDFs have headings and numbered clauses, and answers often miss the relevant clause. Which chunking approach is best?", ["Split every document into fixed 100-token pieces with no overlap", "Embed each whole policy as a single chunk so that nothing is split", "Chunk by heading and clause with section metadata, tuned on a retrieval eval set", "Use very large chunks and raise top-k to 40 so the relevant clause is probably included"], 2, "Structure-aware chunks keep clauses intact and traceable, and an eval set tells you whether size and overlap are right. Fixed or oversized chunks lose or dilute the clause.", "scIN2"),
    S("in008", "integ", "Lookups of exact part numbers such as HX-4420-B return unrelated parts. Which change is best?", ["Add keyword (BM25) search beside embeddings, merge the results, and rerank", "Use a larger embedding dimension so that identifiers are captured much better", "Lower the similarity threshold so more candidates are returned", "Have the model rewrite each query as a plain-language description first"], 0, "Embeddings match meaning, not exact tokens. Keyword search handles identifiers, and a merged, reranked list covers both query types.", "scIN2"),
    S("in009", "integ", "Many policy chunks read 'The Company shall retain records for the stated period' with no policy name, so they match many queries equally. What is the best fix?", ["Raise chunk overlap to 50 percent of each chunk", "Lower top-k so fewer ambiguous chunks reach the model", "Ask the model at answer time to guess which policy each chunk came from originally", "Prepend a short generated document and section description to each chunk"], 3, "Chunks must stand alone. Adding situating context before embedding and keyword indexing, as in contextual retrieval, disambiguates them at indexing time.", "scIN2"),
    S("in010", "integ", "Weekly revisions mean a retired policy version is sometimes cited. Which pipeline design best prevents this?", ["Rebuild the whole index each quarter and tell users to check the document dates themselves", "Use stable chunk IDs with version metadata, upsert changes and delete superseded chunks", "Add a prompt instruction to prefer the newest document whenever two retrieved passages conflict", "Append new versions to the index and rely on recency in the similarity score to rank them"], 1, "Stale content is an indexing problem. Stable IDs, versions and deletion of superseded chunks remove old material; a prompt rule or similarity score cannot do so reliably.", "scIN2"),
    S("in011", "integ", "Users compare values across product specification tables. What is the best handling of those tables?", ["Keep each table whole with its headers", "Split every table into one chunk per row for finer retrieval", "Flatten tables into prose in page order before embedding", "Leave tables out of the index and answer from the surrounding text"], 0, "Row-level chunks lose headers and cannot support comparison. Preserving the table, or querying it as structured data, keeps the relationships the question depends on.", "scIN2"),
    S("in012", "integ", "The team wants to tune chunk size and top-k. What should they build first?", ["A prompt template with more detailed answer-style instructions", "A dashboard of average answer length per day", "A set of labelled queries with the expected chunks", "A larger embedding model, compared by reading a few answers"], 2, "Retrieval quality must be measured separately from generation. A labelled query set makes chunk size and top-k changes testable instead of anecdotal.", "scIN2"),
    S("in013", "integ", "How should the orchestrator integrate the partner's loss-adjuster agent, which runs on the partner's platform and may take hours?", ["Wrap it as a synchronous MCP tool and block the claim until it replies", "Give the partner's agent direct write access to the claims database", "Copy the partner's prompts into the insurer's own subagents", "Use an agent-to-agent task protocol"], 3, "An independently operated, long-running agent calls for task state, status updates and authenticated delegation across the boundary, not a blocking function call or shared database access.", "scIN3"),
    S("in014", "integ", "The adjuster desk needs replies within seconds. Profiling shows intake, fraud-check and policy lookup run one after another and are independent. Which change helps most?", ["Raise the reasoning budget on each subagent to reduce rework", "Parallelise the independent calls and stream", "Move every subagent to the largest available model", "Cache the final answer for each claim so repeat requests are fast"], 1, "Independent steps in sequence add their latencies; parallelising them cuts real time and streaming improves perceived time. Bigger models and more reasoning add time.", "scIN3"),
    S("in015", "integ", "Overnight re-scoring of past claims has no waiting user. What is the best execution mode?", ["Run it through the interactive path at off-peak hours with streaming on", "Reserve extra interactive capacity for the nightly window", "Submit it as batch jobs, trading completion time for lower cost", "Run all claims in one long conversation so context is shared"], 2, "No one waits for each result, so batch processing trades latency for cost. Streaming helps interactive users only, and conversations are not a sharing mechanism.", "scIN3"),
    S("in016", "integ", "Free-text logs cannot explain why one claim received a wrong payout. Which observability change is best?", ["Emit structured traces keyed by a claim ID, tagged with prompt versions used", "Log only the final answer for each claim, together with a timestamp", "Add more verbose free-text messages to each subagent and the orchestrator", "Store daily token totals per subagent and per tool for every workflow"], 0, "A correlation ID and versioned structured traces let you replay the chain of calls behind one outcome. Totals and prose logs cannot.", "scIN3"),
    S("in017", "integ", "At 200,000 claims a day, storing every full prompt and response is costly and holds personal data. What is the best logging policy?", ["Log everything in full and rely on storage lifecycle rules to delete it later, after review", "Log nothing verbatim and keep only the final payout decision", "Sample one percent of traces uniformly and discard the rest", "Keep metrics for all traffic, sample redacted full traces, and retain error traces"], 3, "This keeps cost down and privacy exposure bounded while preserving the rare failing traces needed for diagnosis. Personal-data logging rules should be agreed with the data protection officer.", "scIN3"),
    M("in018", "integ", 2, "Which TWO signals would best reveal a silent quality drift in the payout subagent after a prompt change?", ["Rubric scores on a sample of live outputs, split by prompt version", "The HTTP 200 rate for the payout subagent", "Average request latency across all subagents", "Total monthly spend on the payout subagent", "Rate of human overrides of its recommendations over time"], [0, 4], "Sampled quality scores and human override rates measure answer quality directly. Status codes, latency and spend can all stay flat while quality falls.", "scIN3"),
    S("in019", "integ", "One agent holds 60 tools across billing, HR, deployment and analytics, and tool-choice accuracy has dropped. Which redesign is best?", ["Split into role-focused subagents behind a router", "Keep one agent and add a rule list describing when to use each tool", "Move all 60 definitions into a longer system prompt for clearer ordering", "Permanently disable the 30 least-used tools to halve the catalogue"], 0, "Focused subagents shrink each agent's choice set and its privileges while keeping every capability. Rule lists and longer prompts add tokens without narrowing the choice."),
    S("in020", "integ", "A catalogue contains get_customer, fetch_customer and lookup_customer, each returning slightly different fields. What is the best fix?", ["Keep all three and add 'use the best one' to the system prompt", "Rename them with numeric suffixes so the model can tell them apart", "Merge them into one clearly described tool", "Remove two at random and see whether users complain"], 2, "Overlapping tools are a main cause of wrong selection. One tool with a precise description and field control removes the ambiguity without losing function."),
    S("in021", "integ", "A search tool returns all 20 fields for 50 matches, using thousands of tokens, and the agent loses track of the task. What is the best change?", ["Switch to a model with a longer context window", "Ask the model in the prompt to ignore fields it does not need", "Truncate every result at a fixed byte count before returning it", "Return high-signal fields, with paging and detail by ID"], 3, "Tool results should be concise and high-signal. Paging and on-demand detail keep context small without cutting content blindly."),
    M("in022", "integ", 2, "Which TWO observations most strongly suggest capability bloat in an agent's tool configuration?", ["Selection errors concentrate among tools with near-identical descriptions", "Tool call latency is higher during peak hours of the day", "A large share of input tokens is definitions of tools that are rarely used", "The model sometimes asks a clarifying question before acting", "Tool results are returned as JSON rather than plain text"], [0, 2], "Look-alike tools causing errors and heavy standing cost for unused definitions are the signature of bloat. The others are unrelated to catalogue size."),
    S("in023", "integ", "To save tokens, a team swaps the tool list on every request, adding only tools it predicts are needed, while using prompt caching. Costs rose. What is the most likely cause?", ["Fewer tools always increase output tokens", "Changed tool definitions invalidate the cached prefix", "Caching does not work for requests that include any tools", "Prediction errors force a second request, which doubles the cost"], 1, "Tool definitions sit at the start of the cached prefix, so a changed tool set breaks cache reuse. A stable set, with deferred loading for the tail, is better."),
    S("in024", "integ", "An orchestrator delegates to a research subagent that reads public web pages and a ledger subagent that writes journal entries. How should tools be assigned?", ["Give both subagents the union of all tools so either can substitute", "Give the orchestrator all tools and name the allowed ones in each subagent prompt", "Give each subagent only its role's tools, with writes only on the ledger agent", "Give the research agent write tools too, in case it needs to save findings"], 2, "Role-scoped toolsets reduce selection errors and the blast radius of a compromised or confused agent, which is least privilege applied to agents."),
    S("in025", "integ", "A team wants to prune a 45-tool catalogue. What should it do first?", ["Measure usage and wrong-tool selections on an eval set", "Delete every tool that has not been called during the last week", "Ask the model which tools it finds confusing and remove those", "Shorten every description to a single word to save tokens"], 0, "Pruning should follow evidence. Usage and misselection data identify which tools to merge, defer or remove, and an eval set confirms the change helped."),
    S("in026", "integ", "A remote MCP server for internal data will be shared by 200 staff. Which authentication approach is best?", ["A single API key stored in each user's client configuration file", "A static shared secret that is rotated once a year by admins", "IP allow-listing the corporate network, with no user identity", "OAuth authorization with short-lived tokens tied to each user"], 3, "Shared secrets and network location lose individual identity. OAuth-style authorization with audience-bound, short-lived tokens supports per-user access and revocation."),
    S("in027", "integ", "Employees sign in through SSO to an assistant. One asks it to fetch an HR record they have no right to see. Where must the permission check occur?", ["In the system prompt, which lists the permitted employees", "In the HR tool backend, using the user's identity, on every call", "In the model, which should decline based on the user's role description", "In the SSO login, which already proved who the user is"], 1, "SSO establishes who the user is, not what they may access. The resource must authorise each call in code; prompts and model judgement are not access controls."),
    M("in028", "integ", 2, "Which TWO controls best stop an agent from escalating privileges through its tools?", ["A prompt instruction not to exceed the user's permissions", "Per-tool scopes so each tool can perform only its narrow action", "A broad administrator token, with all calls logged for audit", "Hiding the privileged tool's description so the model cannot see it", "Server-side validation of tool arguments against the caller's permissions"], [1, 4], "Narrow scopes and server-side validation are preventive controls enforced in code. Prompts, hidden descriptions and logging do not prevent a determined or confused call."),
    S("in029", "integ", "A tool needs an API key for a third-party service. Where should the key live?", ["In the system prompt so the model can pass it in tool arguments", "In the tool's input schema as a required parameter", "Held by the backend and kept out of context", "In the first user message of each conversation"], 2, "Anything in the model's context can leak through output or injection. Secrets belong server-side, applied by the backend when it calls the service."),
    S("in030", "integ", "An orchestrator holding admin credentials delegates a refund task to a subagent. Which approach is best?", ["Issue the subagent a short-lived credential scoped to that task and tool", "Pass the orchestrator's admin token to the subagent for speed", "Share one long-lived key among all subagents and rotate it monthly", "Let the subagent call the backend anonymously because the orchestrator is trusted"], 0, "Delegation should narrow, not copy, authority. A scoped, short-lived credential limits what a subagent can do if it errs or is manipulated."),
    S("in031", "integ", "Users say a chat assistant feels slow because replies appear only after the full nine-second generation. Quality is fine. Which change best improves the experience?", ["Switch to a smaller model without evaluating it", "Reduce max_tokens to 200 so replies finish sooner", "Cache the answers to every previous user query", "Stream tokens as generated"], 3, "Streaming shows output immediately, which improves perceived latency without a quality change. Truncating or swapping models unmeasured risks quality."),
    S("in032", "integ", "Traffic is 80 percent simple FAQs and 20 percent complex disputes. Which configuration best balances accuracy, latency and cost?", ["Send all traffic to the largest model for consistent quality", "Route by difficulty: small model for FAQs, large one for disputes", "Send all traffic to the smallest model and accept lower dispute accuracy", "Alternate models at random to average out cost"], 1, "Routing matches capability to need, giving low latency and cost for the common case and accuracy where it counts, with evals proving the split."),
    S("in033", "integ", "A team enables the largest reasoning budget for every request and median latency doubles. What is the best next step?", ["Evaluate by task class and keep extra reasoning only where it pays off", "Disable reasoning everywhere to restore the earlier latency on every task", "Keep it, since accuracy always improves with more reasoning on any task", "Halve max_tokens so the extra reasoning fits in the same time budget"], 0, "More reasoning helps some task classes and wastes time on others. Measure per class and apply it selectively; truncating output risks cutting off answers."),
    S("in034", "integ", "Raising top-k from 5 to 50 lifted recall but added latency and noisy answers. What is the best design?", ["Return to top-k 5 and accept the lower recall", "Keep top-k 50 and tell the model to ignore irrelevant passages", "Retrieve widely, rerank, and pass only the top few", "Use top-k 50 only for premium users"], 2, "A wide first stage keeps recall, and reranking restores precision so the model sees few, relevant passages. This trades a small reranking step for better accuracy and shorter context."),
    M("in035", "integ", 2, "Which TWO changes reduce real latency without harming answer quality?", ["Run independent tool calls in parallel", "Cut max_tokens below typical answer lengths", "Cache the stable prompt prefix, such as instructions and tool definitions", "Remove the verification step that catches wrong tool arguments", "Reduce retrieval to a single passage"], [0, 2], "Parallel calls remove waiting, and caching a stable prefix speeds repeated requests. The others save time by silently reducing completeness or quality."),
    S("in036", "integ", "The SLA is p95 under 3 seconds and at least 92 percent on the eval set. Model A scores p95 1.8 s and 88 percent, Model B 2.6 s and 94 percent, Model C 5.1 s and 96 percent. Which choice is best justified?", ["Model A, because it is fastest and latency is the visible target", "Model B, because it alone meets both targets, backed by recorded eval results", "Model C, because the highest accuracy is the safest choice", "Model A, with a longer prompt to lift accuracy past 92 percent without re-testing"], 1, "The decision is the cheapest option meeting every stated target, supported by measured results. Untested prompt changes and unconstrained accuracy preference are not justifications."),
    S("in037", "integ", "Agent runs sometimes loop far longer than expected. Which data best supports diagnosis?", ["Only the final answer and the total run time recorded for each single task", "Daily request counts and monthly spend across all agent deployments", "The user satisfaction score alone, collected after each finished run", "Per-step tool calls, tokens, latency and stop reason, linked per task"], 3, "Loop problems appear in step-level traces: repeated calls, failing tools, growing context. Aggregates and end results hide where the run went wrong."),
    S("in038", "integ", "A dashboard shows average latency of 2 seconds, yet users report occasional very slow replies. What is the best monitoring change?", ["Track the median instead of the mean", "Lower the dashboard refresh interval", "Track p95 and p99 latency", "Log only the single slowest request each day"], 2, "Averages and medians hide the slow tail. High percentiles, split by route and model, show who is affected and where."),
    S("in039", "integ", "Quality dipped after a release that changed a prompt and a tool description together. Which practice makes diagnosis easiest?", ["Tag traces with prompt, model and tool-set versions so cohorts can be compared", "Roll back everything and promise never to change more than one thing at a time again", "Ask users to report which answers got worse", "Compare this week's average cost with last week's"], 0, "Version tags let you split traces by configuration and attribute a regression to its cause, without relying on memory or anecdotes."),
    S("in040", "integ", "A reviewer asks why full prompts, which can include customer personal data, are kept in logs indefinitely. What is the best response?", ["Explain that logs are internal only and so exempt from data-handling rules", "Redact sensitive fields, limit retention and access, and consult the DPO early", "Stop logging entirely, including operational metrics, to remove the risk", "Encrypt the log store and keep all prompts indefinitely for future audits"], 1, "Observability needs data, but personal data requires minimisation, retention limits and access control, and the right owner (the data protection officer) should be involved. This is not legal advice."),
    M("in041", "integ", 2, "A user reports that the agent updated the wrong order. Which TWO records are most useful for debugging?", ["Total tokens used in the month", "The final answer text only", "The tool name, arguments and result for each call in the run", "A correlation ID linking all steps of the run, with prompt and model versions", "Average latency per day"], [2, 3], "Per-call tool details show exactly which order was targeted and why, and a correlation ID with versions lets you assemble the whole run. Aggregates cannot."),
    S("in042", "integ", "Chunks of 100 tokens match precisely but answers lack surrounding detail, while 2,000-token chunks retrieve noisy results. What is the sound approach?", ["Pick 1,000 tokens as the midpoint between the two sizes and keep it fixed", "Use 100-token chunks and instruct the model to infer any missing surrounding detail", "Always use the largest chunks available, since the model can ignore noise", "Test chunk sizes on labelled queries and expand small chunks to their surrounding text"], 3, "Chunk size is a trade-off to be measured. Retrieving small units and expanding to their context can give both precision and completeness."),
    S("in043", "integ", "A team wants to move to a better embedding model. What is the safest plan?", ["Build a parallel index with the new model, validate it on the eval set, then switch queries over", "Embed only new documents with the new model and keep the old vectors for existing documents", "Switch the query embedding model now and re-embed the documents gradually afterwards", "Re-embed a random 10 percent sample of the documents to check whether results improve"], 0, "Vectors from different models are not comparable, so the whole corpus must be re-embedded. A parallel index validated on evals allows a clean cutover and rollback."),
    S("in044", "integ", "A multi-tenant RAG assistant must show each user only documents they are allowed to read. Which design is best?", ["Retrieve across all documents and ask the model to omit ones the user should not see", "Scan the final answer for restricted keywords before showing it", "Apply the user's permissions as a metadata filter in the retrieval query", "Index only the documents that most users are allowed to read"], 2, "Permissions must be enforced at retrieval, so restricted text never enters the model's context. Post-hoc filtering or model discretion can leak content."),
    M("in045", "integ", 2, "A nightly refresh changes about 5 percent of documents. Which TWO practices keep the index correct?", ["Assign stable document IDs and hash chunks so changed chunks are upserted", "Embed only new documents and ignore edits to existing ones", "Rely on query-time recency boosting instead of updating the index", "Delete chunks belonging to documents that were removed or replaced", "Skip validation after the refresh to keep it fast"], [0, 3], "Stable IDs with hashes let you update only what changed, and deleting superseded chunks stops stale content being retrieved."),
    S("in046", "integ", "Contextual retrieval adds generated context to each chunk before indexing, and a reviewer objects to the extra indexing cost. What is the best-founded response?", ["The cost recurs on every user query, so it should be avoided at any meaningful scale", "It is a one-time indexing cost, so judge it by measured retrieval gain on your own eval set", "It replaces the need for evaluation because the method is already proven in practice", "It works only with keyword indexes, so embedding-based search cannot use it at all"], 1, "The context is generated at indexing time, not per query, and the benefit should be confirmed on your own data. It applies to both embeddings and keyword indexes."),
    S("in047", "integ", "Analysts ask 'total revenue by region for Q3' over a sales table. Which retrieval strategy is best?", ["Embed each row and retrieve the 20 most similar rows", "Chunk the table into text and use hybrid search", "Load the whole table into the prompt for every question", "Give the agent a read-only query tool, such as SQL"], 3, "Aggregations are computed, not retrieved. A query tool gives exact results, while similarity over rows cannot sum or group reliably."),
    S("in048", "integ", "A coding agent must find every caller of a function named parseInvoice in a large repository. Which approach fits best?", ["Exact search such as grep, then reading the matches", "A vector index of code chunks that is rebuilt monthly by a scheduled job", "Asking the model to recall likely callers", "Summarising the repository into one document"], 0, "Exact symbol lookups suit exact search, which is always current. A stale vector index or recalled guesses can miss callers."),
    S("in049", "integ", "A question such as 'Which vendor supplies the part used in the product we recalled in March?' needs one lookup to feed the next. Which retrieval pattern fits?", ["A single retrieval with a very large top-k", "A single retrieval using the full question as one embedding query", "An iterative, step-limited loop", "Pre-compute answers for every possible pair of facts"], 2, "Multi-hop questions depend on intermediate answers, so iterative or agentic retrieval fits. A step limit keeps the loop bounded."),
    S("in050", "integ", "A 40-page FAQ is stable and relevant in every conversation. What is the simplest effective design?", ["Build a vector database and retrieve the top-k chunks for each query", "Place the whole 40-page FAQ in the system prompt as a cached static prefix", "Train a custom model on the FAQ and redeploy it after each update", "Summarise it to a paragraph each month and place that in the prompt"], 1, "A small, stable corpus does not justify retrieval infrastructure. A cached prefix is simple, fast and complete, and you can add retrieval when the corpus grows."),
    M("in051", "integ", 3, "Which THREE are sound reasons to add hybrid retrieval (keyword plus embeddings) with reranking?", ["Queries mix exact identifiers and natural-language paraphrases", "The corpus holds fewer than 20 documents", "Keyword and vector rankings fail on different queries, so merging improves recall", "You wish to avoid evaluating retrieval", "All queries are aggregations over a table", "A reranker can place the best few passages first before they reach the model"], [0, 2, 5], "Hybrid search helps mixed query types and complementary failures, and reranking improves what the model actually sees. Tiny corpora and aggregations call for other approaches."),
    S("in052", "integ", "Questions ask 'which services depend, directly or indirectly, on the payments database?' Which approach is best?", ["Embed the service documentation and retrieve the closest passages to the question", "Raise top-k over the architecture wiki so that more related pages reach the model", "Run full-text search on the word 'payments' across the repositories and wiki", "Model service dependencies as a graph or relational structure and query it by traversal"], 3, "Transitive relationships are a traversal problem. Text similarity or keyword search will miss indirect dependencies."),
    S("in053", "integ", "One internal app must call a single stable REST service for a fixed workflow, and no other AI application will use it. What is the proportionate integration?", ["Define a tool in the app that wraps the call", "Build, host and secure a standalone MCP server", "Use an agent-to-agent protocol between two agents", "Have the model write raw HTTP requests at runtime"], 0, "With one consumer and one stable backend, a direct tool wrapper adds no extra server to run. MCP pays off when several applications reuse the capability."),
    S("in054", "integ", "The same ticketing capability must be used by Claude Code, a desktop assistant and an internal agent. What is the best approach?", ["Hand-write a separate integration in each application", "Share a REST API and let each team write its own tool definition", "Expose it once as an MCP server that all three can connect to", "Use an agent-to-agent protocol so each application delegates to a ticketing agent"], 2, "Write-once reuse across several MCP-capable clients is what MCP standardises. Separate integrations duplicate effort, and a ticketing function does not need a delegated agent."),
    S("in055", "integ", "In a Claude Code workflow, the agent needs git and docker operations on a developer's machine. What is the best integration?", ["An MCP server wrapping every git and docker subcommand as separately typed tools", "Run the existing command-line tools directly through the shell, within permission rules", "An agent-to-agent protocol to a git specialist agent", "Paste command output into the prompt by hand"], 1, "Familiar CLIs need no schemas and add little context cost, and permission rules bound what they may do. A wrapper server adds overhead for little gain here."),
    S("in056", "integ", "One MCP server gives file access on each developer's own machine, while another provides shared company search for all staff. Which transports fit?", ["Streamable HTTP for the local one and stdio for the shared one", "stdio for both, since it is the simplest", "Streamable HTTP for both, leaving the local one without any authentication because it is local", "stdio for the local server, Streamable HTTP with authorization for the shared one"], 3, "stdio runs a local server as a child process, while a shared remote server uses Streamable HTTP and needs authorization."),
    S("in057", "integ", "One application contains an orchestrator and three specialist subagents in the same process. A colleague proposes an agent-to-agent network protocol between them. What is the best view?", ["Use the framework's in-process subagent mechanism, and keep network protocols for external agents", "Adopt the protocol for every agent, including internal ones, since it is the standard approach", "Replace the subagents with separate MCP servers so each one has its own network endpoint", "Run each subagent as a separate hosted service to improve isolation between the specialists"], 0, "Agent-to-agent protocols solve cross-boundary delegation. Inside one application they add network hops, failure modes and operations work with no benefit."),
    M("in058", "integ", 2, "Which TWO characteristics favour an agent-to-agent protocol over a plain tool?", ["The capability is a single stateless lookup that returns in milliseconds", "The remote party is an independently operated agent that reasons and acts on its own", "The data is a static reference document", "The work is long-running and needs task status, updates or input requests over time", "The call needs no authentication because both ends share a machine"], [1, 3], "Autonomous remote agents and long-running, stateful tasks are the cases agent-to-agent protocols are designed for. Simple lookups are tool calls."),
    S("in059", "integ", "A team exposes a function that creates a ticket, a read-only product catalogue the application attaches as context, and a reusable 'triage this ticket' template a user selects. Which MCP primitives fit?", ["All three as tools", "Catalogue as prompt, template as resource, function as tool", "Function as a tool, catalogue as a resource, template as prompt", "Function as resource, catalogue as tool, template as prompt"], 2, "Tools are model-controlled actions, resources are application-controlled context, and prompts are user-selected templates."),
    S("in060", "integ", "A team has 30 specialised procedures, each needed only occasionally. Which design uses progressive discovery well?", ["One system prompt containing all 30 procedures in full for every request", "Package each as a skill with short descriptions and on-demand detail", "Load all 30 procedures in full only when the user types a special keyword", "Send all 30 as the first user message of every new conversation session"], 1, "Skills expose only brief metadata up front and load instructions when relevant, so the standing cost stays low while the capability remains available."),
    S("in061", "integ", "A 40-page procedures manual in the system prompt fits the window, but answers are slower and costlier and sometimes skip relevant steps. What is the best change?", ["Move it to the end of the prompt", "Repeat the key steps three times", "Switch to the largest context window available, to be safe", "Keep an index in context; load sections on demand"], 3, "Large irrelevant context raises cost and latency and can dilute attention. An index with on-demand loading keeps context small and relevant."),
    S("in062", "integ", "When is a monolithic context the better choice?", ["The content is small, stable and needed on almost every request", "The content is large and only a few percent of requests use any of it", "The content changes every few minutes", "The content is spread across thousands of documents"], 0, "Small, always-needed, stable content costs little in a cached prefix and avoids extra round trips. The other cases favour on-demand loading or retrieval."),
    S("in063", "integ", "An agent exploring a large repository keeps pasting whole files into context and loses focus. Which approach follows just-in-time retrieval?", ["Preload the whole repository into the prompt at the start", "Summarise the repository once and never open files", "Keep file paths and open files on demand", "Restart the conversation after every file"], 2, "Holding lightweight identifiers and loading content when needed keeps context focused on the current step."),
    S("in064", "integ", "After deferring all 50 tools behind tool search, latency rose and simple tasks became less reliable. What is the best adjustment?", ["Remove the search tool and load everything again", "Keep the most-used tools loaded and defer the rest", "Defer the search tool itself too", "Add a longer system prompt describing every deferred tool"], 1, "Progressive discovery adds a lookup step, so hot-path tools belong in the stable prefix. At least one tool, normally the search tool, must stay non-deferred."),
    S("in065", "integ", "A research task needs dozens of documents read to answer one question, and the main agent's context is crowded. What fits progressive discovery best?", ["Have the main agent read every document directly", "Increase max_tokens so the answer can include all details", "Give the subagent the main agent's full history so it has context", "Delegate to a subagent that returns a concise summary"], 3, "A subagent isolates the exploratory reading and returns only the distilled result, keeping the main context small."),
    M("in066", "integ", 2, "Which TWO practices make a progressive-discovery index effective?", ["Short descriptions that state what each item does and when to load it", "Putting the full instructions in every description so nothing needs loading", "Keeping deeper reference files one link away from the main instructions", "Using generic names such as 'helper' and 'utils'", "Hiding the index so the model must guess what exists"], [0, 2], "Clear when-to-use descriptions let the model decide to load more, and shallow reference structure keeps loading predictable. Full text in descriptions defeats the purpose."),
    S("in067", "integ", "A compliance bot needs three short rules in every answer and consults 500 reference documents occasionally. What is the best arrangement?", ["Rules in the cached prompt; documents retrieved on demand", "All 503 items in the system prompt", "All 503 items retrievable on demand, including the rules", "Rules in the retrieved set and documents in the system prompt"], 0, "The always-needed core belongs in the stable prefix and the large occasional corpus in on-demand retrieval, combining both strategies.")
  );

  Object.assign(window.CCA.why, {
    "in001": ["A larger model does not remove the cost of 48 definitions, and overlapping names still invite mistakes. It treats a catalogue design problem as a capability problem.", "Measuring usage, merging overlapping tools and deferring rarely used servers reduces definition tokens and the number of look-alike choices together, and can be validated against an eval set.", "Examples for 48 tools would make the prompt larger and more expensive without fixing the overlap that causes the confusion.", "max_tokens limits output length and does not help the model choose between tools or reduce the size of the input definitions."],
    "in002": ["Logging is a detective control and it still records the shared account, not the person. The over-broad access remains.", "A prompt instruction is not an access control. It can be bypassed by error or injection and the CRM would still allow broad reads.", "Rotation reduces the window for a stolen secret but leaves the over-broad shared access and the loss of identity untouched.", "Carrying the real user's delegated identity and checking permissions in the CRM enforces least privilege and preserves attribution."],
    "in003": ["The server should accept only tokens meant for it and use its own downstream credential. This is the audience-validation and no-passthrough rule that prevents confused-deputy abuse.", "Encryption protects transit but not the underlying problem: the downstream API trusts a token that was never issued for it.", "A shorter lifetime narrows exposure but keeps the forbidden passthrough, so the audit trail and audience binding stay broken.", "Relocating the forwarding does not create a token meant for the billing API, and the client still sends a credential the downstream service should not accept."],
    "in004": ["A prompt cap is a request, not a barrier. Error or injected text can lead the model to exceed it.", "A nightly report finds the problem after the money has moved. It is detective, not preventive.", "A limit enforced on the server and an approval step above a threshold stop the action regardless of what the model decides.", "A second model call is probabilistic and can be manipulated in the same way, so it is not a reliable gate for a financial action."],
    "in005": ["One long description block still costs tokens on every request and does nothing to reduce confusion between similar tools.", "Deferred loading keeps the tools available through search while removing their standing token cost for most sessions.", "Giving every subagent all tools maximises bloat and blast radius, which is the opposite of the goal.", "Exposing servers only to roles that need them shrinks both the catalogue each agent sees and the privileges it holds.", "Larger budgets pay for the bloat rather than removing it, and selection quality can still suffer with many overlapping tools."],
    "in006": ["A prompt line is easily overridden by crafted text and is not a structural control, so the credit tool stays exposed.", "Marking ticket text untrusted, separating read from write and confirming writes limit what injected text can cause. It is least privilege plus a human gate.", "Keyword stripping is trivially evaded by rephrasing, and it would damage legitimate tickets.", "Training reduces but does not eliminate injection success, and monitoring alone finds harm after the fact."],
    "in007": ["Fixed tiny pieces cut clauses mid-sentence and lose headings, so the relevant clause is still often missed.", "A whole policy as one chunk is too coarse, so unrelated text dilutes the embedding and wastes context.", "Clause-aware chunks with section metadata keep meaning intact and support citation, and the eval set confirms the size and overlap choices.", "Large chunks plus a high top-k add noise, latency and cost and do not make the right clause easier to find."],
    "in008": ["Keyword search matches the exact identifier, and merging with embedding results then reranking handles both exact and semantic queries.", "Vector dimension does not make embeddings reliable at matching arbitrary alphanumeric strings.", "A lower threshold returns more candidates but does not make the exact part rank higher, and adds noise.", "Rewriting the query into prose usually removes the exact identifier that the user needs matched."],
    "in009": ["Overlap repeats text but does not name the policy, so the ambiguity remains while the index grows.", "Fewer chunks reduces noise but may drop the right one, and ambiguous chunks still cannot be told apart.", "At answer time the model has nothing reliable to infer the source from, so it would be guessing.", "Situating context added before embedding and keyword indexing lets each chunk match the right policy. This is the contextual retrieval approach."],
    "in010": ["A quarterly rebuild leaves up to a quarter of stale answers and puts the burden on users.", "Stable IDs, version metadata, upserts and deletion of superseded chunks remove retired content at the source.", "A prompt preference for newer documents cannot work if the retired chunk is the only one retrieved.", "Appending without deleting leaves old chunks in place, and similarity has no notion of which version is current."],
    "in011": ["Keeping the table whole with its headers, or querying it as structured data, preserves the relationships a comparison needs.", "Row chunks lose column headers and cannot support comparison across rows or products.", "Flattened prose obscures which value belongs to which column and breaks side-by-side comparison.", "Dropping tables removes exactly the content the comparison questions ask about."],
    "in012": ["Style instructions affect how an answer reads, not whether the right chunk was retrieved.", "Answer length says nothing about retrieval quality and cannot guide chunk size or top-k.", "Real queries with expected chunks let you measure recall at k directly and compare settings objectively.", "A bigger model changes several things at once, and judging by a few examples is anecdotal rather than measured."],
    "in013": ["A blocking call suits a short stateless function, not work that may take hours and needs status updates.", "Direct database write access crosses a trust boundary and breaks least privilege without solving coordination.", "Copying prompts does not reproduce the partner's agent, tools or data, and it is not integration.", "A task-based agent protocol gives discovery, lifecycle and asynchronous status, with authentication appropriate to a separate organisation."],
    "in014": ["More reasoning raises latency on every step and does not remove the sequential waiting.", "The calls are independent, so running them concurrently cuts wall-clock time, and streaming improves how fast the answer appears.", "The largest model is usually slower, which works against a few-second target.", "Claims differ and rarely repeat identically, so caching final answers helps few requests and does not fix first-time latency."],
    "in015": ["Streaming only improves perceived speed for a waiting person, and running interactive-priced traffic overnight wastes the cost trade-off.", "Reserving interactive capacity pays for low latency that no one needs in this workload.", "With no waiting user, batch processing exchanges completion time for lower cost, which matches the requirement.", "The API is stateless and one long conversation would grow the context and mix unrelated claims."],
    "in016": ["Structured, versioned traces linked by a correlation ID let you reconstruct which steps, tools and versions produced the outcome.", "A final answer and timestamp say nothing about the steps that led to it.", "Verbose prose is still hard to search and correlate across components.", "Daily totals are aggregates and cannot explain one specific claim."],
    "in017": ["Retaining everything is expensive and increases personal-data exposure even if deleted later.", "Keeping only decisions removes the evidence needed to debug errors and review quality.", "Uniform one-percent sampling will often miss the rare failures that matter most.", "All-traffic metrics, redacted sampled traces and guaranteed retention of failures balance cost, privacy and diagnosability."],
    "in018": ["Scores on live samples, split by prompt version, directly show whether the change altered quality.", "A successful status only shows the call completed, not that the payout recommendation was good.", "Latency is a performance signal and can stay constant while answers get worse.", "Spend can stay flat while output quality changes, so it is not a quality signal.", "A rising override rate shows humans disagreeing more with the subagent, a direct proxy for degraded recommendations."],
    "in019": ["Role-focused subagents behind a router cut each agent's choice set and privileges while preserving all capabilities.", "A rule list adds tokens and complexity while the agent still faces 60 choices.", "A longer prompt does not narrow the choice and increases input cost on every request.", "Disabling half the tools by usage alone removes capability that some tasks need, and nothing shows the cut helps."],
    "in020": ["A vague prompt line does not tell the model how the tools differ, so misselection continues.", "Numeric suffixes carry no meaning, so the model still cannot decide which to use.", "One well-described tool removes the overlap and keeps the field coverage through a parameter.", "Random removal risks breaking real use cases and replaces measurement with complaints."],
    "in021": ["A longer window pays for the verbose output and does not reduce the noise that makes the agent lose track.", "The tokens are already spent once the tool returns them, so asking the model to ignore fields saves nothing.", "Cutting at a fixed byte count can drop the useful part and break structure.", "High-signal defaults, paging and detail by ID keep results small yet complete when needed."],
    "in022": ["Near-identical descriptions causing misselection is a core sign that the catalogue needs consolidation.", "Peak-hour latency points to load or capacity, not to the number or overlap of tools.", "Paying tokens each request for rarely used definitions is the cost side of bloat.", "Clarifying questions are normal behaviour and unrelated to the size of the tool set.", "Result format is a design choice and says nothing about how many tools exist."],
    "in023": ["The number of tools has no systematic link to output tokens, and the cost change is explained by caching.", "Tool definitions are part of the cached prefix, so changing them per request invalidates and rewrites the cache.", "Tools can be cached; the problem is changing them between requests, not having them.", "A retry after a wrong prediction would add some cost, but the cache invalidation from changing definitions on every request is the more direct cause."],
    "in024": ["The union gives both agents more reach than they need and removes the benefit of role separation.", "Naming allowed tools in a prompt is not enforcement, and the subagents still see the whole set.", "Each subagent gets only what it needs, and keeping write capability on one agent limits harm.", "Adding writes to the research agent widens its privileges for a speculative need and increases exposure to injected web content."],
    "in025": ["Usage and misselection data tell you what to merge, defer or remove, and an eval set verifies the result.", "A one-week window ignores tools used rarely but critically, such as month-end tasks.", "The model's opinion is not evidence of real selection errors or usage.", "One-word descriptions would remove the information the model needs to choose correctly."],
    "in026": ["Keys in client configs are shared secrets that are hard to revoke per person and easy to leak.", "A static shared secret has no per-user identity and a long exposure window.", "Network location says nothing about the person and fails for remote or compromised hosts.", "OAuth-style authorization issues short-lived, audience-bound tokens linked to a user, enabling per-user access and revocation."],
    "in027": ["A prompt list is not an enforceable control and can be bypassed.", "The backend that owns the record must check the user's rights on each call, in code.", "Model judgement can be manipulated, and role text in the prompt is not authoritative.", "Login confirms identity only, which is authentication and not permission for a specific record."],
    "in028": ["A prompt instruction is advisory and can be bypassed by error or injection.", "Narrow scopes mean a tool cannot do more than its defined action, even if misused.", "A broad token maximises what a mistaken call can do, and logs detect harm only afterwards.", "Hiding a description does not remove the capability and offers no real protection.", "Validating arguments against the caller's permissions on the server blocks out-of-scope requests regardless of what the model sends."],
    "in029": ["Prompt content can be echoed in output or extracted by injection, so a key there is exposed.", "A required parameter makes the model handle the secret and exposes it in logs and traces.", "The backend applies the secret when calling the service, so the model never sees it.", "A message in the conversation is context the model can repeat or leak."],
    "in030": ["A task-scoped, short-lived credential limits what the subagent can do and for how long.", "Passing the admin token gives a subagent the orchestrator's full power and is a classic escalation path.", "A shared long-lived key makes every subagent equally powerful and hard to attribute.", "Anonymous calls bypass authentication, so the backend cannot attribute or authorise anything."],
    "in031": ["An unevaluated model change risks quality loss and does not address why replies seem slow to arrive.", "A low max_tokens may truncate answers, harming quality for a modest gain.", "Previous queries rarely repeat exactly, so caching helps few requests and not the wait on new ones.", "Streaming shows text as it is produced, which greatly improves perceived responsiveness."],
    "in032": ["The largest model for everything pays latency and cost on simple FAQs that do not need it.", "Routing sends each request to a suitable model, and evals confirm the quality and latency of each path.", "All-small saves cost but gives up accuracy on the high-stakes disputes.", "Random alternation ignores difficulty, so hard cases may get a weak model and easy ones an expensive one."],
    "in033": ["Selective use by task class keeps the accuracy gains where they matter and removes needless latency elsewhere.", "Disabling everywhere throws away accuracy gains on tasks that benefit.", "More reasoning does not always help; it costs time and tokens and can add little on simple tasks.", "Cutting max_tokens may truncate answers and does not make the extra reasoning faster."],
    "in034": ["Going back gives up recall that real queries need, rather than recovering precision.", "Noise still costs tokens and latency and can mislead the model even when told to ignore it.", "Wide retrieval for recall and reranking for precision gives the model few strong passages.", "Tiering by user does not fix the design flaw and creates uneven quality."],
    "in035": ["Independent calls that run concurrently remove waiting without changing results.", "Cutting max_tokens truncates answers and trades quality for speed.", "A stable prefix cached across requests reduces repeated processing time and cost.", "Removing verification increases wrong tool arguments, which is a quality loss.", "One passage lowers recall and risks missing the answer."],
    "in036": ["Model A misses the accuracy target, so it fails the SLA however fast it is.", "Model B meets both p95 and accuracy, and recording the eval evidence justifies the decision.", "Model C misses the p95 target by a wide margin, so accuracy alone cannot justify it.", "Lifting accuracy by prompt edits is plausible but unproven, and skipping re-testing leaves the SLA unverified."],
    "in037": ["The end result shows nothing about repeated calls or failures inside the run.", "Daily and monthly aggregates cannot show what one run did.", "A satisfaction score is a lagging, coarse signal with no cause attached.", "Per-step traces linked per task reveal repetition, failing tools and growing context behind a long loop."],
    "in038": ["The median also hides the slowest few percent that users notice.", "Refresh frequency changes how often you see the number, not what it shows.", "High percentiles expose the slow tail, and segmentation shows which routes or models cause it.", "A single slowest request a day is a sample of one, not a distribution."],
    "in039": ["Version tags allow cohort comparison and attribution of the dip to one of the two changes.", "Changing one thing at a time is good practice but does not help diagnose the change already shipped.", "User reports are slow and anecdotal and rarely identify cause.", "Cost trends do not show quality changes."],
    "in040": ["Internal logs holding personal data still need minimisation, retention and access rules, so the claim is wrong.", "Redacting sensitive fields, limiting retention and access, and consulting the data protection officer address the issue while keeping logs useful.", "Stopping all logging removes the observability needed to operate and debug the system.", "Encryption protects storage but does not address indefinite retention or minimisation."],
    "in041": ["Monthly token totals say nothing about which order was touched.", "The final text omits which order the tool actually received.", "The recorded tool name, arguments and results show which order was targeted and what happened.", "A correlation ID with versions connects every step and shows which configuration produced the call.", "Daily latency is unrelated to a wrong-target error."],
    "in042": ["A midpoint is arbitrary and may serve neither precise nor complete answers.", "The model cannot reliably infer detail that was never retrieved, so it may invent it.", "Large chunks add noise, cost and latency, and the model can still be misled by it.", "Size should be tuned empirically, and retrieving small and expanding to context can combine precision with completeness."],
    "in043": ["A parallel, validated index and a clean cutover allow rollback, and avoid mixing incompatible vectors.", "Mixed models in one index give incomparable vectors, so search quality becomes unpredictable.", "A query model that differs from the document model produces meaningless similarities during the transition.", "A sample shows little about corpus-wide quality and does not provide a migration."],
    "in044": ["The model would already have seen restricted text, and omission is not reliable.", "Keyword scanning of output is easily evaded and cannot judge what a user may read.", "A permission filter at retrieval means unauthorised content never reaches the model, so it cannot be leaked.", "Limiting the index denies legitimate users access to documents they may read."],
    "in045": ["Stable IDs and hashes let the refresh update only changed chunks reliably.", "Ignoring edits leaves outdated chunks in the index.", "Recency boosting cannot remove stale chunks and may not rank the updated one first.", "Removing chunks of replaced or deleted documents stops retired content being retrieved.", "Skipping validation lets a broken refresh reach users unnoticed."],
    "in046": ["The context is generated once at indexing, not on each query.", "It is a one-time indexing cost, which caching can also reduce, and your own eval set shows whether it pays off.", "A published benefit does not remove the need to test on your data.", "It applies to both embedding and keyword indexes."],
    "in047": ["Similar rows are not a sum by region, so the result would be wrong.", "Text chunks lose the table's structure and cannot reliably aggregate.", "Loading the whole table wastes context and still asks the model to do arithmetic.", "A read-only query tool lets the database compute exact aggregates, which is the right tool for the data shape."],
    "in048": ["Exact search finds every textual reference and is always current with the repository.", "A monthly index is stale between rebuilds, and semantic similarity can miss exact names.", "Recall from training data is a guess and cannot know this repository.", "A summary omits detail and cannot list all callers."],
    "in049": ["A bigger top-k still cannot supply information that depends on an earlier answer.", "One query for the whole question cannot retrieve facts that only become known after the first step.", "An iterative loop uses each result to form the next query, and a step limit keeps it bounded.", "Pre-computing pairs is infeasible and goes stale."],
    "in050": ["A vector pipeline adds infrastructure and the risk of missing chunks for a corpus that fits easily in context.", "A cached static prefix is simplest and complete for a corpus this small.", "Custom training is heavy, slow to update and unnecessary for a small FAQ.", "A tiny summary would lose the detail the FAQ exists to provide."],
    "in051": ["Mixed exact-token and paraphrase queries are the textbook case for hybrid search.", "A tiny corpus can usually go in context, so hybrid retrieval is unnecessary.", "Complementary failure modes mean merging the rankings raises recall.", "Skipping evaluation is not a reason, and hybrid systems also need to be tested.", "Aggregations over tables need a query tool, not hybrid text retrieval.", "Reranking improves the ordering so the model sees the best few passages."],
    "in052": ["Documentation passages rarely list indirect dependencies, so many will be missed.", "A higher top-k adds noise and still cannot compute transitive links.", "The word 'payments' appears in many unrelated places and says nothing about dependency.", "A graph or relational model can follow direct and indirect edges exactly, which matches the question."],
    "in053": ["A direct wrapper is the simplest design for one consumer and one stable backend.", "A standalone MCP server adds hosting, security and maintenance burden when nobody else will reuse it.", "No second agent is involved, so an agent protocol is a mismatch.", "Raw runtime HTTP generation is unreliable and bypasses authentication and validation."],
    "in054": ["Three integrations triple the effort and drift apart over time.", "A shared REST API still leaves each team writing and maintaining its own tool definitions.", "One MCP server serves every MCP-capable client with a single implementation.", "A ticketing function is a plain capability, not an autonomous agent, so delegating adds needless indirection."],
    "in055": ["A wrapper server for every subcommand costs context and maintenance for tools the model already knows.", "Existing CLIs are familiar to the model, cost almost no schema tokens, and permission rules bound their use.", "A git function is not an independent agent, so a protocol adds overhead without benefit.", "Manual pasting removes automation and is error-prone."],
    "in056": ["This reverses the fit, putting a remote protocol on the local server and a local one on the shared service.", "stdio cannot serve many users over a network from a shared server.", "Streamable HTTP for a local server is unnecessary, and omitting authentication is unsafe whichever transport is used.", "stdio suits a local process and Streamable HTTP with authorization suits a shared remote service."],
    "in057": ["In-process subagents avoid network overhead, and cross-boundary protocols are kept for agents run by others.", "Using a network protocol everywhere adds latency and operational cost with no interoperability gain inside one application.", "Turning subagents into servers adds endpoints and hops without need.", "Separate services add deployment and failure modes beyond what the requirement demands."],
    "in058": ["A stateless millisecond lookup is a plain tool call.", "An independent agent that reasons and acts on its own is a peer to delegate to, which is the agent-protocol case.", "A static document is a resource or retrieved context, not a delegated task.", "Long-running work needing status and updates matches the task lifecycle that agent protocols provide.", "Skipping authentication is not a design reason, and local calls still need access control."],
    "in059": ["Not everything is model-controlled action, and read-only context and user-selected templates have their own primitives.", "This swaps the resource and prompt roles, which are application-controlled context and user-selected templates respectively.", "An action the model invokes is a tool, application-attached read-only context is a resource, and a user-selected template is a prompt.", "A function that acts is a tool, not a resource, and catalogue data is context, not an action."],
    "in060": ["All 30 in full costs tokens on every request, even though each is needed only occasionally.", "Short metadata up front and full instructions on demand give low standing cost with available detail.", "A keyword trigger is brittle and does not let the model decide what is relevant.", "Sending everything in every conversation is the monolithic pattern, with the same cost."],
    "in061": ["Position does not remove the cost or dilution of a large irrelevant context.", "Repetition lengthens the prompt and does not make the right section easier to find.", "A larger window allows more text but does not cure slowness, cost or dilution.", "A brief index with on-demand sections keeps context small and relevant."],
    "in062": ["Small, stable, always-needed content is cheap as a cached prefix and avoids lookup round trips.", "Rarely used large content should be loaded on demand, not carried in every request.", "Frequently changing content defeats caching and suits retrieval.", "Thousands of documents cannot fit and need retrieval."],
    "in063": ["Preloading a large repository floods the context and costs far more than needed.", "Never opening files removes the detail the agent needs.", "Lightweight references loaded on demand keep focus and context small.", "Restarting loses the progress made and the context that matters."],
    "in064": ["Reloading everything restores the original token cost and the selection problems.", "Keeping the hot tools loaded avoids needless lookups, and deferring only the tail keeps savings.", "At least one tool must remain non-deferred, so deferring the search tool breaks discovery.", "A long description of every deferred tool recreates the token cost that deferral was meant to remove."],
    "in065": ["Reading everything directly fills the main context with raw material.", "A larger max_tokens affects output length and does nothing for crowded input context.", "Passing the full history defeats the isolation that makes subagents useful.", "A subagent explores in its own context and returns a distilled summary, protecting the main agent's focus."],
    "in066": ["Clear descriptions of what and when let the model decide whether to load more.", "Full instructions in every description defeat progressive loading and bring back the token cost.", "Shallow, one-hop references keep loading predictable and complete.", "Generic names give the model no basis for choosing.", "A hidden index leaves the model unaware of what it could load."],
    "in067": ["Always-needed rules in the cached prefix and occasional documents on demand fit each content type to a strategy.", "Putting 500 documents in the prompt wastes tokens on every request.", "Rules left to retrieval might not be retrieved, so they would sometimes be missing from answers.", "This inverts the arrangement, leaving required rules unreliable and the prompt bloated."]
  });
})();
